<h2 id="about">What this tool does</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><p>An EMI — equated monthly instalment — is the fixed amount you pay each period so that a loan is exactly repaid at the end of its term. It comes from the standard formula EMI = P·r·(1+r)ⁿ ÷ ((1+r)ⁿ − 1), where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of payments. This page runs that calculation for you and, unlike a bare formula widget, also breaks the loan down year by year so you can see how much of each payment is interest versus principal.</p><p>The early part of almost any loan is mostly interest. On a five-year personal loan the first year might be three-quarters interest, and only in the final months does principal repayment dominate. The amortisation table here makes that visible: for each year it lists the opening balance, the interest paid, the principal paid and the closing balance, so you can plan an early part-payment around the years that would save you the most.</p><p>Because loans are quoted in different rhythms, you can choose monthly, quarterly or annual payments and enter tenure in years or months. Pick a currency and every figure is formatted with the right symbol. When you want the full schedule for a spreadsheet or a bank form, one button exports it as CSV — the numbers are computed locally and nothing you enter is uploaded.</p></div>
<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use the EMI Loan Calculator <span style="color:var(--cy)">| Step by Step Guide</span></h2>
<div class="howto"><ol><li><span>Enter the loan principal and the annual interest rate.</span></li><li><span>Set the tenure and choose whether it is in years or months.</span></li><li><span>Select monthly, quarterly or annual payment frequency, and a currency.</span></li><li><span>Press Calculate to see the EMI, total interest, total payment and the yearly amortisation table.</span></li><li><span>Press 'Download CSV' to export the full payment schedule.</span></li></ol></div>
<div class="t3d-textbox ru" data-ru lang="ros"><p>Loan ka amount, saalana byaaj %, tenure (saal ya mahinay) aur frequency (monthly/quarterly/annual) likhein. 'Calculate' dabane par EMI, kul byaaj, kul qeemat aur saal-ba-saal amortization table milegi — poori schedule CSV me download bhi kar sakte hain.</p></div>
<h2 id="features">Features</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul class="feat-list"><li>Correct EMI formula with a graceful zero-interest path</li><li>Monthly, quarterly and annual payment frequencies</li><li>Tenure entered in years or months</li><li>Total interest and total repayment shown up front</li><li>Year-by-year amortisation table with opening and closing balances</li><li>CSV export of the complete schedule and seven currency formats</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
<div class="t3d-textbox"><ul><li>Compare the interest column across years before deciding to prepay — early years carry the most interest.</li><li>A 1% drop in rate saves more over a long tenure than you expect; re-run to quantify it.</li><li>Keep EMIs under roughly 40% of monthly income — raise the tenure if the number is too high.</li><li>Use 'annual' frequency only for loans that genuinely bill once a year, not to sanity-check a monthly EMI.</li><li>Export the CSV and add it to a spreadsheet before negotiating a balance transfer.</li></ul></div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>What EMI formula does this use?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>The reducing-balance formula EMI = P·r·(1+r)ⁿ ÷ ((1+r)ⁿ − 1), where r is the periodic rate (annual rate divided by payments per year) and n is the total number of payments.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Why is early EMI mostly interest?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Interest each period is a percentage of the outstanding balance, which is highest at the start. Principal repaid per instalment therefore grows over the loan — the amortisation table shows the crossover.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Does a longer tenure save or cost me money?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>It lowers each instalment but raises total interest, because the balance stays high for longer. The total-interest line makes the trade-off explicit.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Is the emi loan calculator free to use?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>Yes — EMI Loan Calculator has no usage cap, no sign-up and no watermark, and it is not a trial: the page is a static file on https://wafasmart.tech with no billing backend behind it. </p></div></details>
<details><summary>Are processing fees or insurance included?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>No — this is the pure amortised-loan maths. Add bank fees and insurance outside it to get the true cost of borrowing.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Does anything get uploaded when I use emi loan calculator?</summary><div class="t3d-textbox"><p>No. EMI Loan Calculator reads your loan figures straight into page memory, works on it locally and hands the result back as a download — there is no upload request to intercept, store or leak, and no log of what you processed.</p></div></details>
